Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

241
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Once a Scout has completed all the other requirements

242
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
he needs for a rank,

243
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,000
he is left basically with two.

244
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
One of them is a Scout Masters conference

245
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
and the other one is a Board of Review.

246
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Now, we've talked about Boards of Review

247
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
and things like that, and as a Scout Master,

248
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
you don't get to participate in Boards of Review,

249
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
but you do get to do Scout Master conferences.

250
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
As a matter of fact, you get to do

251
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
Scout Masters conferences

252
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
and then you get to participate in the Board of Review.

253
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
As a matter of fact, you get to do

254
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
just about all the Scout Master conferences.

255
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
so it's an important piece of the job

256
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
that we do need to discuss from time to time.

257
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
Now, why do we engage in this kind of one-on-one review?

258
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Well, the relationship between a Scout and a Scout Master

259
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
is important for the troop's health

260
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
and for the troop's success.

261
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,000
The Scout Master needs to watch the troop dynamics

262
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
to see who's showing leadership

263
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
and who's holding back,

264
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
who might be a little shy

265
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
with the younger Scouts, who's skilled in outdoor activities, etc.

266
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
Further, the Scout Master is watching

267
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
the individual Scout to determine

268
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
whether he's advancing, whether he's having fun

269
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,000
and whether he seems eager or uneasy.

270
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Now we always emphasize that

271
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
this is not an opportunity for a Scout Master

272
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
to sit and retest a Scout's skills.

273
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
I think that's the greatest single misdirection

274
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
experienced in many troops.

275
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
Once we are disabused of the misconception

276
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
that we should be retesting or testing a Scout

277
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
during a Scout Master's conference,

278
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
we can move on to more important business.

279
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
A Scout Master conference is really just a chance

280
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
to talk to a Scout about how he's doing,

281
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
about how he feels about the troop

282
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
and his patrol and his role in it,

283
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
how he's advancing

284
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
and how he's striving to live up to the ideals of Scouting.

285
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Most importantly, the Scout Master conference

286
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
can be a way of encouraging the individual Scout.

287
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
The Scout Master conference is the most personal method

288
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
in Scouting to assess the needs and desires of a Scout

289
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
and then to encourage and support him

290
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:53,000
to learn of his hopes and his fears

291
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
and to help him see himself

292
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
in the greater context of Scouting

293
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
and to encourage his personal growth,

294
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,000
both in skills and in living up to the Scout oath and law.

295
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:06,000
There are a couple of things that I commonly end up

296
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
assessing at a Scout Master's conference.

297
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,000
One is Scout spirit

298
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
and one is active participation

299
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,000
in the Scout's troop and patrol.

300
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
And there's a very simple way of doing this,

301
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
and one that has never failed me,

302
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
and that is I simply ask the Scout to assess themselves.

303
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,000
You know, we look at the requirement that says

304
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
this requirement here says: you know,

305
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
you must show Scout spirit.

306
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
Give me some examples about how you've done that.

307
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:38,000
Tell me, if you were going to sign this for yourself,

308
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
would you be able to?

309
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
Do you think you've completed that requirement?

310
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
I do the same thing with the active part.

311
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
I just say: you know,

312
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
do you consider yourself to be an active Scout?

313
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Why do you consider that?

314
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,000
Can you defend that proposition to me there?

315
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:00,000
And that usually is a way that we can jump off

316
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
into talking about other subjects,

317
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
talking about other things.

318
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:10,000
I don't necessarily confine the subject of my questions

319
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:13,000
or of the conference itself to Scout stuff.

320
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
I like to ask about family.

321
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
I like to ask about school.

322
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
You know what's your favorite subject in school?

323
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Is there anything that you really struggle with?

324
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
What did your grades look like last time?

325
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:26,000
Were they pretty good,

326
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
You know, get along pretty good with your brothers

327
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
and sisters, little things like that.

328
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
A lot of younger Scouts are a little more cowed

329
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
and tasked to turn at Scoutmaster conferences.

330
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
Frankly, you know,

331
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
I know that they're kind of scared of me.

332
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
I asked my senior patrol leader about this

333
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
several months ago

334
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and you know I just said,

335
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
why are the younger Scouts scared of me?

336
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
And he didn't skip a beat.

337
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
He said: well, you have a big beard,

338
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
a loud voice and an off-putting manner.

339
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:59,000
Yeah,

340
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:02,000
So I try to be nice and personable.

341
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:08,000
I don't holler and scream or, you know, act badly.

342
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
But for some reason

343
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
the younger Scouts are a little frightened of me

344
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,000
and we just try and work it out as best we can.

345
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
Sometimes it's hard to get them to speak,

346
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:22,000
but if you ask lots of good, open-ended questions

347
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
they will calm down.

348
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
You know you have to remember that most boys

349
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
who are Scout age are not going to have

350
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
regular informal conversations

351
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
with adults outside their family.

352
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
When you think about it, you know

353
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
that's not going to happen all that often,

354
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
So it might take them some time

355
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,000
to get comfortable.

356
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
I think a lot of times Scouts

357
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
are looking for the right answer,

358
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
as if they believe I'm trying to trip them up,

359
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:52,000
and it often takes two or three conferences

360
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:53,000
before they understand

361
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:57,000
there aren't any right answers involved.

362
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
I've found that if I begin the conference

363
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
by asking about subjects outside of Scouting,

364
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
they're more likely to open up

365
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
when we start talking about Scouting things.

366
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
Conferences really need to last

367
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:11,000
about as long as they ought to last-

368
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
Five to ten minutes,

369
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
I think that's plenty,

370
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
And you should be able to develop

371
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,000
a good idea of what a Scout is thinking

372
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:24,000
and how he's experiencing his troop and patrol

373
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
and how he's experiencing Scouting in general.

374
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
You should be able to suss out

375
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
if there are any real difficulties.

376
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
You should be able to get a profile

377
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
of his skills, interests and abilities

378
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
And you can do that

379
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
within five or ten minutes.

380
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
I find my briefest conferences

381
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
are for those Scouts who are,

382
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
you know, Star, Life and Eagle.

383
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:45,000
to tell you the truth,

384
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
Eagle is usually a very brief conference

385
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
because by the time a Scout has reached that point

386
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
in his Scouting career,

387
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
not just because of age or experience,

388
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
but you know, if he's going to be

389
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
a Star and a Life Scout

390
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
and then become an Eagle Scout,

391
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
he's going to spend a fair amount of time

392
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
invested in the leadership of his troop and patrol

393
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
And that means that he and I

394
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
are going to talk on a pretty regular basis

395
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
And I'm going to know an awful lot

396
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
about what he's doing and thinking

397
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
because we're going to have

398
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
pretty much an ongoing conference

399
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
during those years

400
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
that he's part of a troop.

401
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
It's hard for me to sit and ask questions.

402
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
I already know the answers to.

403
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
You know for the most part.

404
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
So, finally,

405
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
the last part of the Scout Masters Conference,

406
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
before I sign the book and send them on their way,

407
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
is: do you have any questions for me

408
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
And is there anything

409
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
that you need to know about

410
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
or you're uncertain about as far as the troop

411
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
or your patrol is concerned, or how things work?

412
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
And you know, let me know

413
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
if there's anything that you need

414
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
me to help you with,

415
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
And it works very well that way.

416
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
So let's just review quickly.

417
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
a Scout Masters Conference is a good

418
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
informal conversation in which you get a profile

419
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
of the Scout and his experience

420
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
and his patrol and his troop and his scouting in general.

421
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
You can find out

422
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
a lot about them. You can find out

423
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
a lot about their interests and what they're thinking.

424
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
You can

425
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
find out about what's going on

426
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
within their patrol. You can find out

427
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
what's going on in the troop. that way,

428
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
It's usually a pretty

429
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
enjoyable time.

430
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
Younger Scouts will be a little

431
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
cowed, They won't know exactly how to deal with

432
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
this and, you know, despite

433
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
every explanation and

434
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
reassurance that you give them, they

435
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
will continue to be that way, Maybe

436
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
for a conference or two, until

437
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
they settle down a little bit and

438
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
it becomes somewhat easier for them.

439
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
So, and then

440
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
end the conference

441
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
by asking the Scout if

442
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
they have any questions.

443
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
Five or ten minutes should do it.

444
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
Don't make it a big official, difficult

445
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
thing. No forms, no papers,

446
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
please. No

447
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
tests, no quizzes.

448
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
I think you'll find that it's one of

449
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
the more informative and

450
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
more enjoyable tasks that

451
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
you have as a Scout leader.

452
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
Hi, this is Dr

453
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
Paul Auerbach and you are

454
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
listening to Clark Green

455
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
on the Scoutmaster Podcast.

456
00:18:38,000 --> 00:19:00,000
Well, if

457
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
you're listening to this, you probably have a teenage child

458
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
living in your home, Maybe a couple.

459
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
My sympathies.

460
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
No, it's an exciting time of life.

461
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
It sure is a lot of fun

462
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and it goes by very quickly.

463
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
My teenage child is now

464
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
28 and you know,

465
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
moving along, doing great,

466
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
But every once in a while

467
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
I want to look at him

468
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
and I want to say: you know, if you just knew

469
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
what was going to happen in the next 15 or 20

470
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
years. and if you just

471
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
listen to what I tell you,

472
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:34,000
Yeah, it's a

473
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
yeah,

474
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
that's never going to work.

475
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
It's never going to work, but I'm going to give it a try

476
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
anyway. I'm going to give it a try with you

477
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
because I hear from a lot of people who

478
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
are becoming new

479
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
Scoutmasters- you know, becoming

480
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
new volunteers and scouting-

481
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
And I want to tell you- and I

482
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
sometimes look at the things that you're thinking

483
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
and you're saying and you're doing- and I say, yeah,

484
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
you know, if you just knew,

485
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
if you just knew what it was going to be like

486
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
in five or ten years, you know.

487
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
So here's

488
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
a try. I'm really

489
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
honestly going to give this a try

490
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
Because if I could speak to myself

491
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
27 years ago when I

492
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
became a Scoutmaster, here's what I

493
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
would say. I'd say

494
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
this: learn as much as you can

495
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
from training and reading.

496
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
Study the Guide to Safe Scouting

497
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
and the Advancement Committees and Policies

498
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
and Procedures Manual and the Insignia Guide,

499
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
the Scoutmaster's Handbook Especially.

500
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
look at Baden-Powell's AIDS to Scout

501
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
Mastership and memorize

502
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
the Introduction to the Scout

503
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
Handbook, Because

504
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
that's where all

505
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
of the promises that are made to

506
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
a Boy Scout when he becomes

507
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
a part of our organization are enumerated

508
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
right there in that first

509
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
chapter. I would tell myself that

510
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
you will encounter any number of

511
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
cynical old Scouters,

512
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
loads of misinformation

513
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
and a few real renegades who've recreated

514
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
Scouting in their own image. Don't admire

515
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
them, don't imitate them, do not believe all

516
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
that much of what they say.

517
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
I would tell myself that

518
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
the culture will change but the basic

519
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
motivations, desires and needs

520
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
of my Scouts are going to remain the same,

521
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
And that

522
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
the real genius of Scouting is that it

523
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
recognizes and addresses those basic,

524
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
unchanging needs. Whatever new

525
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
form they have to take on, the fears

526
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
and concerns and criticisms of fellow leaders

527
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
and parents and committee members will remain

528
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
the same.

529
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
Almost every imaginable conflict

530
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
and problem in Scouting has

531
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
already been met and resolved by someone,

532
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
So lean on the advice

533
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
and direction of experienced help.

534
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
As much fun

535
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
as it is for you to play the game,

536
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
remember that you are the coach

537
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
and you're not allowed to get in the game.

538
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
You have to let the Scouts play it on their own.

539
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
I would tell

540
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
myself that Scouts will advance and

541
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
participate at their own speed and in their own time.

542
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
As they learn to lead,

543
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
they'll make a lot of mistakes.

544
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
Your personal standard of performance

545
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
can't be applied to them, only the standards

